<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: everyone does this</h2>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">The task: Chennai from 1985 to now</div>
    <p>Open the class Google Earth project, <a href="https://earth.google.com/web/@13.0913,80.2829,33a,11900d,35y,0h,0t,0r/data=ChYqEAgBEgoxOTg1LTEyLTMxGAFCAggBMikKJwolCiExeEpXeUtPYkRDZU9lZVFOUG1XRmxRVks5VVAyVDhpdkwgAToDCgEwQgIIAEoICPmap70HEAE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EES Chennai Over Time (1985 to 2026)</a>. Use Chrome and your AISC account. It opens on the coast at Chennai Port with the 31 Dec 1985 satellite image showing.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Close the popups. The large "New from Google Earth" box closes with the X; the small tip boxes close with Dismiss. More than one appears, so keep closing them until the map is clear.</li>
      <li>In Map contents on the left, double-click <strong>Stop 1</strong>. Look at the 1985 image first. Then click the <strong>&gt;|</strong> button at the top to jump to the newest image. Then switch the Surface water layer off and on.</li>
      <li>Do the same at <strong>Stop 2</strong>.</li>
      <li>Record two observations at each stop in your Class Notebook, using the table below. Describe what you see before you try to explain it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Stop 1: Chennai Port and Marina Beach coast</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Find the long walls that stick out into the sea at the port. What shape are they?</li>
      <li>Compare the sand south of the port with the coast north of the port. Use the ruler to measure the width of the beach in at least two places on each side.</li>
      <li>Is the beach width the same in 1985 and in the newest image? Measure the same spots in both.</li>
      <li>Turn on Surface water. Where along the coast do you see light blue, and where do you see a sharp edge?</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Stop 2: The mouth of the Cooum River</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Look closely at the place where the river meets the sea. What is between the river and the ocean?</li>
      <li>Trace the river back into the city. Is its path straight or curved? How is it shaped around Fort St. George?</li>
      <li>Compare the color and width of the river in 1985 and in the newest image.</li>
    </ul>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>#</th><th>Stop</th><th>Image date</th><th>What I see (facts only)</th><th>Evidence (measurement or screenshot)</th><th>Process that might cause this</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>In the last column write weathering, erosion, deposition, human activity, or "not sure". "Not sure" is a fine answer today.</p>
    <p><strong>Be a careful observer</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The 1985 image is blurry. Compare only features big enough to see in both years: rivers, coastlines, the color of the land.</li>
      <li>The newest image is stitched from photos taken on different days. A color change along a straight edge is usually the stitching, not a real change on the ground.</li>
      <li>Read the date at the bottom of the screen every time and write it down. The date of the newest image changes with how far you zoom in.</li>
      <li>Screenshots must show that date.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want support</div>
    <p><strong>Using the tools.</strong> Historical imagery is the bar across the top of the map: <strong>&lt;</strong> and <strong>&gt;</strong> step to an earlier or later image, and <strong>&gt;|</strong> jumps to the newest. Surface water is in Map contents: hover over it and click the eye icon to hide or show it. Dark blue means water was there almost all the time across many years; light blue means water only some of the time. The Surface water layer does not change when you move the timeline, so hide it when you want to see the photo underneath. The ruler icon in the toolbar measures distance: click a start point, then an end point. If double-clicking a Stop does not move the map, type Chennai Port or Cooum River in the search bar.</p>
    <p>Lost? This labeled image shows the same stretch of coast, north up.</p>
    <figure class="content-figure">
      <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-chennai-harbor-s2cloudless-2016.jpg" alt="Satellite image of the Chennai coast from about 13.03 N to 13.17 N, north up. Marina Beach is the wide sand at the bottom, the Cooum mouth is just above it, Chennai Port is in the middle, and a narrow, groyne-lined shore runs north from the fishing harbor.">
      <figcaption>The Chennai coast, north up. Sentinel-2 cloudless, <a href="https://s2maps.eu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://s2maps.eu</a>, by EOX IT Services GmbH (Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2016 &amp; 2017), CC BY 4.0. Limitation: this is a mosaic of the clearest scenes from 2016 and 2017, so it has no single date.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Waves reach the beach at an angle, so each swash carries sand up the beach on a slant and the backwash pulls it straight back down. Over thousands of waves the sand walks along the coast: longshore drift. Chennai's harbor breakwaters block that walk, which is why Marina, to the south, is wide and the shore to the north erodes.</p>
    <figure class="content-figure">
      <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-earle-17-1-8-longshore-drift.png" alt="Diagram of a breaking wave meeting a sandy beach at an angle. Arrows trace one sand grain's path: up the beach on a slant with the swash, straight back down with the backwash, repeated in a zigzag that moves the grain along the shore.">
      <figcaption>Figure 17.1.8, the movement of particles on a beach by swash and backwash. © Steven Earle, <a href="https://opentextbc.ca/physicalgeology2ed/chapter/17-1-waves/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Physical Geology</em>, 2nd ed.</a>, CC BY 4.0.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a href="https://www.ck12.org/earth-science/Landforms-from-Wave-Erosion-and-Deposition/lesson/Landforms-from-Wave-Erosion-and-Deposition-HS-ES/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Easier reading: CK-12, Landforms from Wave Erosion and Deposition</a>. Beaches, spits and sea cliffs at a gentler reading level. Pairs with Earle 17.2 and 17.3.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want to go further</div>
    <p>Double-click <strong>Stop 3</strong> (Pallikaranai Marsh and the lakes of south Chennai). With Surface water on, count the dark blue and the light blue patches. Then hide Surface water and compare the land around the lakes in 1985 and in the newest image.</p>
    <p>Or find your own Landscape Reading location and record one thing that changed between 1985 and the newest image. Or compare this coast with Mahabalipuram, using the Shore Temple as your marker.</p>
  </div>
